good afternoon everyone  i'm pleased to be here to provide an  update on covet 19 in alberta  dr hinshaw is taking some much deserved  time off this week and so is unable to  attend today's update we'll but we'll be  back next week  today the numbers i'm sharing are from  tuesday march 22nd to monday march 28th  as always the detailed breakdown of data  is available at alberta.ca  let me begin by talking about two key  leading indicators that help us assess  the level of viral transmission in the  province positivity rates and waste  water surveillance  for march 22 to 28 our daily positivity  rate of pcr tests range from 21.7  to 27.1  with an average of 24.5 percent this is  a slight increase from the 22  average in the previous seven days  the wastewater surveillance data also  shows signs of rising transmission  a number of sites across the province  including edmonton and calgary are  showing initial signs of increasing  trends however as we know  wastewater data can be highly variable  so we will continue to monitor these  levels and other leading indicators in  the coming days and coming weeks  given that we have lifted the vast  majority of public health measures over  the last seven weeks it is not  unexpected that we are seeing a slight  increase in transmission of covet across  the province  there are simply more opportunities for  the virus to spread as more and more  people work from the office a return to  traveling socialize in various settings  and resume their regular routines  this is especially true as ba2 omicron  subvariant is a dominant strain in  alberta and is shown to be more  transmissible than ba1 our lab has found  that around 70 percent of all positive  pcr test samples are now ba ii  fortunately the available evidence does  not so far suggest that it causes more  severe illness than the previous omicron  strain  at this point we continue to have  capacity in the health system to address  the other surgical and medical needs of  albertans  since our update last week  hospitalizations have remained stable  there are currently six or sorry  964 people in hospital with coven 19  including 47 in the icu  sadly  lives are still being lost to this  disease over the last week 30 deaths  related to coven 19 have been reported  to alberta health  this averages just over four per day  my deepest sympathies go out to the  loved ones of these albertans my  sympathies are also with anyone who has  lost someone they care about no matter  the cause  even as we transition  our approach to managing covid we know  that the virus is still very much with  us it can still cause serious illness  complications and even death for some  albertans  that's why we continue to promote  vaccination  encourage individuals to make decisions  that best suit their unique situation  and risk tolerance  and invest in treatments like paxalopin  as you may recall paxlovit is the first  covenanting treatment approved by health  canada that can be taken orally at home  it was first approved for use in canada  in late january but due to limited  supply it was initially only available  at 135 pharmacies across the province  i'm pleased to report that as supply has  increased beginning this friday any  interested pharmacy in alberta will be  able to order paxlova treatments to  dispense paxloved to eligible albertans  who have met the clinical criteria  as a reminder  albertans who meet the eligibility  criteria which is available only by  prescription must call healthlink to  access the treatment  staff will ask screening questions to  confirm eligibility and arrange the next  steps for getting the most appropriate  covent 19 treatment  please do not call pharmacies or  physicians directly to get a  prescription as they will be unable to  give it to you  paxlovin must be started within five  days of symptoms so if you are if you  are eligible do not wait to see how  things develop  call healthlink as soon as possible  if you aren't sure if you are eligible  visit the ahs website to read the  criteria  while paxlova continues to be an option  to prevent mild or moderate cases of  covet 19 from regressing to severe  disease it is not a substitute for  vaccination  vaccination remains the most effective  way to prevent severe cases of covet 19  and reduce the risks that come with  getting infected so if you have not  received every dose available to you  please do so as soon as possible  if you have a child that still needs a  coven 19 vaccine dose try to take  advantage of the last day of extended  ahs clinic hours and walk-in  appointments tomorrow  a complete list of clinic locations and  hours is available  on the ahs website now if you can't make  it tomorrow don't worry  many pharmacies clinics and physician  offices across alberta can administer  the pediatric vaccines by appointment a  complete list again is available on the  blue cross website  finally i want all i want to encourage  all albertans to pick up rapid test kits  from their local pharmacy  as we're out and about and exposed to  more people it's a good idea to have  tests on hands if we develop symptoms  the kits are free and it's easier to get  now more than ever as you no longer need  to show your alberta healthcare card to  pick them up  once again i'd like to thank all of our  healthcare  providers our doctors our nurses those  working in hospitals and volunteers for  continuing to provide tremendous care  for all albertans through  the the last two years and through the  most recent omicron wave  thank you and now i'll take questions  thanks minister uh no one in the room  here with us uh but i think a couple of  reporters waiting on the phone so we'll  go to the first caller please  just track city news  hi there uh just wondering you know with  v8 ii is the dominant strain now and you  were just uh mentioning that we are  seeing sort of small increases in trends  that do you think we're going to enter a  sixth wave or have we already entered a  sixth wave  uh we're going to have to wait and see  you know if we look at  other jurisdictions  around the world and across the the  country we they you know we have seen  an increase in ba2 like an increase in  in cases  what remains to be seen is is how big  that wave how big that wave will be  and then what the impact will be on our  on our health care system um but you  know as indicated by um  dr tam and canada there's an expectation  is that we may see a rise in cases uh  but we're still gonna have to see what  that may it how that may impact our  health care center or our health care  system i i can say we there's you know  our wastewater data  is sort of the  one of the leading indicators we are  seeing a slight uptick initial uptick in  particularly in calgary and everton but  there's high variability in that so  we're going to have to watch and  over the next days and weeks to see  whether or not that is going to continue  to rise  thanks minister jeff follow follow-up  uh yeah a lot of people i know who  recently become sick say that it's kind  of hard to get their hands on a free  rapid testing kit i was responding what  what are supplies that are we are are we  still okay or are we running low on on  those kids  yeah i don't know the exact numbers uh  in front of me we can get that to you my  understanding is is that we have uh  significant supplies that are in  that we have distributed to uh to  pharmacies across the province but we'll  get you the the exact numbers i haven't  heard any concerns yet about supplies  and and but we'll we'll get you the  numbers and then we'll take a look at  the uh ensure that those numbers are  also updated on the website  yeah i will follow up separately jeff  thanks for that so uh we'll go to the  next caller please  stephanie thomas ctv  hi minister coughing well you did  mention that covet wastewater sampling  that's showing an increased presence of  the virus especially in calgary  so i'm wondering if perhaps your office  or dr henshaw's office has some other  data that you're looking at that might  link that rise in the case detection and  the wastewater to hospitalization is  that something you're seeing  yeah we're not we're actually seeing  hospitalizations um either being stable  or continue to trend downwards so we're  not seeing a link at this point in time  thanks follow-up stephanie  yeah so if it was to show that we are  headed to i suppose another wave with  severe outcomes including  hospitalizations what measures is your  office looking at implementing  well we're going to have to wait and see  first in terms of whether there is going  to be a uh be first of all is this going  is this are we at the start of another  wave one uh and the two what is the  impact on on hospitalized  hospitalizations um i do know and  again you look at other jurisdictions um  they  you know they have had uh an increase in  ba2 which you know indicated there was a  slight bump in uh in  cases in the community um and also a  small bump in hospitalization so there  wasn't a need to actually take any  measures at that point in time uh so  we're gonna quite frankly we're gonna  have to wait and see we'll watch there's  some other jurisdictions that are ahead  of us on this i will watch what they are  doing and then we'll see  you know our own indicators of what  actions we may or may not have to take  but but again it goes back to um what is  the severity going to be i you know i am  comforted to some regard some regards um  from a in a couple of from a couple of  points first  we we have relatively high levels of  vaccination uh you know for those who  are 12 plus you know over 90 percent  with uh uh with at least one dose and  and second doses and third doses are  continuing to uh to go up uh there is  some population protection from a  previous infection uh from va uh ba1 and  we have access broader access to uh  treatment sites such as paxlovit so  again we're watching very carefully uh  the numbers and we're looking at other  jurisdictions to assess the risk  thanks minister we'll go to the next  caller  elena smith canadian press  hi mr copping i'm hoping to provide an  update for us based on alberta's  strategy for the rollout of fourth doses  yeah i think  dr hinshaw spoke about fourth doses last  week or at this point in time  for only the  the  you know potentially for the  immunocompromised and not going anything  further we will rely on the  the expert advice from  the national uh committee  on immunization as well as our our  alberta committee as well  follow up elena  sure yeah i'm also just wondering um are  you getting any reports of folks getting  fourth doses who aren't yet eligible and  are those numbers of who's gotten fourth  doses available online  so i have to double check we we are  tracking those who are actually getting  uh fourth doses um and uh so we actually  have those exact numbers so we can get  them to to you i my recollection is that  um given that we don't have a total  population of what is quote unquote  amino compromise we may not have a good  percentage  of that or it may not be as accurate but  we can get you the exact numbers of four  doses  thanks for that minister uh we'll take  one more caller  so we'll go ahead with the last caller  calgary herald  i think my question um i'm just  wondering if doctor henshaw wasn't  available for  questions today why wouldn't the  province's wdc's medical officer of  health made available to answer  questions  well quite frankly we didn't we didn't  feel it was uh necessary for today dr  hinshaw will be back uh next week and if  there are any uh detailed questions that  we need to provide answers to  we can get them through our  steve  and through the cmoh's office and they  can provide it  thanks minister brittany follow-up  thank you i'm just going to transmission  increasing uh what advice does the  project have  right now for people as they're uh  planning their upcoming daughters  so so again  um you know we are moving to a system of  of individuals living with covet  so our first set of advices is you know  we we ask you know albertans to assess  their own risk right uh understand you  know um  what their level of comfort is uh and  then be guiding accordedly accordingly  as well as you know  go get rapid tests as indicated earlier  today if you haven't uh  gotten all your  booster shots that you're eligible for  please do so  and then and then continue to um listen  for updates um from you know our offices  as we go forward but but again you know  as we move into a position where we're  learning to live with covid you know  asking individuals to  manage their own risk  and be able to take actions  accordingly  thanks very much minister and thanks for  reporters who called in we'll wrap up  there and see you next week thanks very  much  you

